{{smaller block|<poem>In her fair cheek,
Where several ''worthies'' make one dignity.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., iv. 3. 236.}}}}

'''The nine worthies.''' {{smaller|See ''nine.''}}

'''worthy†''' (wėr′ᴛ̵ʜi), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''wurthen'', ''worthien'', ''wurthien'', < AS. ''weorthian'', ''wyrthian'', ''wurthian'' (= OHG. ''werdōn'', G. ''würdigen'' = Icel. ''virtha'' = Goth. ''wairthōn''), value, < ''weorth'', worth: see ''worth''<sup>2</sup>, ''a.''] To render worthy; exalt.
{{smaller block|<poem>Put upon him such a deal of man,
That ''worthied'' him.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, ii. 2. 128.}}}}

'''wortle''' (wėr′tl), ''n.'' '''1.''' A draw-plate, or the aperture in such a plate through which wire is drawn.
{{smaller block|The wire [of manganese steel], owing to its hardness, breaking into short lengths when being pulled through the ''wortles.''
{{right|''Science'', XII. 286.}}}}

'''2.''' One of a series of metal collars through which a cylinder or plug of lead is sometimes drawn in the manufacture of lead pipe. {{smaller|The wortles are of graduated sizes, and the lead is passed from one through that next smaller, till the pipe has acquired the desired size.}}

'''wort-refrigerator''' (wėrt′rẹ̄-frij″ẹ-rā-to̤r), ''n.'' A wort-cooler.

'''wortwale†''' (wėrt′wāl), ''n.'' [Origin obscure.] A hangnail.
{{smaller block|''Pipitula'', the skinne growing at the fingers ends about the nayle, called of some the ''wortwales'', or liuereages.
{{right|''Florio'', 1598.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
= OHG. ''wunt'', G. ''wund'' = Goth. ''wunds'', wounded; possibly orig. pp. (in ''-d''<sup>2</sup>) of the verb which appears in AS. ''winnan'' (pp. ''wunnen''), strive, fight, suffer: see ''win''<sup>1</sup>, ''v''. The historical pron. is wound, parallel to that of ''ground'', ''found'', ''sound'', ''bound'', etc.] '''1.''' In ''surg.'', a solution of continuity of any of the tissues of the body, involving also the skin or mucous membrane of the part, caused by some external agent, and not the result of disease.
{{smaller block|<poem>I, lately caught, will have a new made ''wound'',
And captive-like be manacled and bound.</poem>
{{right|''Marlowe'', tr. of Ovid’s Elegies, ii.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''medical jurisprudence'', any lesion of the body resulting from external violence, whether accompanied or not by rupture of the skin or mucous membrane—thus differing from the meaning of the word when used in surgery. {{smaller|Great difference of opinion, however, appears in the way in which the word is interpreted when occurring in criminal statutes. Some authorities have held that it necessarily implies the use of a hard or solid instrument other than the hand or fist; others, that it necessarily implies the breaking of the skin beyond the cuticle or outer membrane.}}
'''3.''' A breach or hurt of the bark and wood of a tree, or of the bark and substance of other plants.—'''4.''' Figuratively, injury; hurt; harm: as, a ''wound'' given to credit or reputation, feelings, etc.: often specifically applied in literature to the pangs of love.
{{smaller block|<poem>Alas, poor shepherd! searching of thy ''wound'',
I have by hard adventure found mine own.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, ii. 4. 44.}}}}
{{smaller block|The ''wounds'' of conscience, like other ''wounds'', though}}

