ludes to a supposed vulnerary property.—'''2.''' The kidney-vetch, ''Anthyllis vulneraria'', and occasionally other plants.—{{smaller|'''Clown’s woundwort.''' Same as ''clownheal''.—'''Knight’s woundwort''', the water-soldier, ''Stratiotes aloïdes''. See ''Stratiotes''.—'''Saracen’s woundwort.''' See ''Saracen’s comfrey'', under ''Saracen''.}}

'''woundworth''' (wönd′wėrth), ''n.'' A composite plant, ''Liabum Brownei''. [West Indies.]

'''woundy<sup>1</sup>''' (wön′di or woun′di), ''a.'' [< ''wound''<sup>1</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Causing or inflicting wounds. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>A boy that shoots
From ladies’ eyes such mortal ''woundy'' darts.</poem>
{{right|''Hood'', Love.}}}}

'''woundy<sup>2</sup>''' (woun′di), ''a.'' [Of doubtful origin; perhaps a colloq. use of ''woundy''<sup>1</sup>; cf. ''whopping'', ''terrible'', and other words of intensity, used as emphatics.] Excessive. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Indeed there is a ''woundy'' luck in names, sirs,
And a main mystery. ''B. Jonson'', Tale of a Tub, iv. 2.</poem>
A ''woundy'' hinderance to a poor man that lives by his labour.
{{right|''Sir R. L’Estrange.''}}}}

'''woundy<sup>2</sup>''' (woun′di), ''adv.'' [< ''woundy''<sup>2</sup>, ''a.''] Exceedingly; very. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|A ''woundy'' brag young yellow.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Tale of a Tub, i. 2.}}
Gad, says I, an you play the fool and marry at these years, there ’s more danger of your head’s aching than my heart.—He was ''woundy'' angry when I gav ’n that wipe.
{{right|''Congreve'', Love for Love, iv. 13.}}
Travelled ladies are ''woundy'' nice.
{{right|''J. Baillie.''}}}}

'''wourali, wourari''' (wö′ra-li, -ri), ''n.'' Same as ''woorali'', ''woorari''. See ''curari''.
